statement.text:
I2-F1 authority-only foundation: exact-head review of 29f5b2cd. Primary: integration results note 97c8bfff. This review rests on all 31 artifacts, one per path changed against approved fc8e2093. Verdict: approved, as an internal foundation only. Full I2 stays open.

What I ran. I used a scratch clone at 29f5b2cd with npm ci and a build, on Node 26.10.0.
- tests/native-authority.test.ts plus tests/native-wire.test.ts passed 55/55.
- The retained-fixture replay (replay-retained.mjs over public-vectors.json.gz) produced JSON exactly equal to the retained node26-retained.json: the same 54 cases, the same fixture hashes, and whole snapshots and outcomes.
- The change adds only the three application authority modules, tests and evidence. There is no graph, package or export change against fc8e2093.
- I did not rerun Chromium or Node 22.19; they are retained evidence.

Architecture: sound and matches the adopted NW0, I2 and N1 decisions.
- Capability boundary:
  - accepted state and authenticated entries are WeakMap brands minted only by openNativeAuthority, interpretNativeAuthority and authenticateAuthorityEntry;
  - nativeAuthoritySnapshot returns an owned clone, and nothing parses a snapshot back into a brand;
  - interpretNativeAuthority works on a clone and mints new state only on return, so any thrown missing, invalid or runtime fault leaves the prior state intact.
- Ordinary act and activate throw content_unavailable, so no eligibility exists without source admission. There is no caller callback.
- Context and uniqueness (assertNativeAuthorityContext):
  - the exact next position and predecessor;
  - unique request CIDs;
  - unique retry tuples for signed requests;
  - single-use descriptors.
  These are checked both before authentication and again during interpretation.
- Control precedence follows NW0 and its D5 correction:
  - a context or tip that is stale, then the signer is not appointed, then a missing power;
  - recover is required for setRecovery, recoverGovernance and recoverParticipant, and govern for everything else;
  - setControl can neither add recover nor alter or remove any recover-containing pair (its exact powers are preserved);
  - setRecovery is non-empty (schema minimum 1) and replaces only recover bits, keeping lower powers;
  - recoverGovernance needs the immutable genesis flag and replaces only govern bits;
  - more than 16 pairs gives control_map_limit;
  - the tip advances only on an effective change.
- Roles follow G/null/disabled:
  - genesis roles start enabled with revision G;
  - never-assigned roles have expected revision null;
  - a disable keeps its intent CID;
  - a no-op gives role_unchanged;
  - assignRole needs a live grant (admitted, matching CID, not revoked, current epoch, matching signer), the owner, and the role in scope.
- Account operations:
  - the floor advances even when the change is ineffective, with rollback and conflict detected by TID order and root;
  - expectation gives authority_stale;
  - revocation before admission produces the null-CID revoked row, and later admission gives grant_revoked;
  - epoch progress needs a chain whose oldest new transition's previous is the current epoch, with no reused CIDs or IDs.
- Evidence authentication (native-authority-evidence.ts):
  - the appointed observation policy, and the app's before and after identity bound to the publication repository key, with web only when policy allows it;
  - genesis and entry bytes proven under the authenticated app root;
  - the descriptor's exact app, genesis, position, predecessor and subject (nativeObservationSubject);
  - method routing fixed by the policy (PLC directory path or the hostname did:web URL), with the I1 binding unchanged from before to after;
  - proof CARs authenticated under the observed key and the expected root;
  - exactly the required subject paths, nothing unrelated;
  - an epoch walk from the current pointer to the expected anchor, bounded by the 16-record schema cap and refusing cycles;
  - authenticated absence is invalid, and a missing block is unavailable.
- Appointed recovery replaces a participant floor only with never-accepted epochs and the exact prior expectation. A failed recovery mutates nothing. This matches the documented and adopted rule.

Security
- No public constructor, fromJSON, trust boolean or permission callback.
- The publication key must equal the retained app method binding.
- A parsed snapshot is data only, and failures preserve the prior state.
- The trust limits are disclosed: web fabrication by the app PDS, single-recover-key replacement, and possibly blocked rotation at map pressure.

Simplification: none required.
- Minor: authenticateAuthorityEntry checks the observation-policy shape twice (configuredPolicy, then policy). Drop the second check.
- For P4 (my P4D2-1): the snapshot's requests, retries and consumedObservations arrays are exactly what the checkpoint compact projection proposes to derive from history. Keep the I2 snapshot format as is. The compact checkpoint shape is a separate projection.

Remaining, as stated: source admission and eligibility, activation, atomic Folder integration, publication, the observer, checkpoint restore and a fresh Chromium run on this base.
